index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..e659d2f55ef3c885a8c1fc34e343efc6aebe1a42
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/ShareXLocalFeatures.md
@@ -0,0 +1,175 @@
+# ShareX-inspired local feature patch
+
+This patch set adds local editing and selection features to Flameshot while
+keeping Linux as the primary platform. It is based on upstream commit
+`c886a35b`.
+
+## Apply the single patch file
+
+Start from a clean clone of the official repository. The patch contains the
+complete commit series, including this guide.
+
+```sh
+git clone https://github.com/flameshot-org/flameshot.git
+cd flameshot
+git switch --create sharex-local-features c886a35b
+git am --3way /path/to/flameshot-sharex-local-features-c886a35b.patch
+```
+
+If the exact base commit is not present in a shallow clone, fetch the full
+history first. Do not apply the patch on top of an unrelated or modified tree.
+
+## Build on Linux
+
+The upstream minimums are Qt 6.2.4, GCC 11, and CMake 3.22. Install the normal
+Flameshot dependencies for the target distribution.
+
+Debian and Ubuntu:
+
+```sh
+apt install g++ cmake build-essential qt6-base-dev qt6-tools-dev-tools qt6-svg-dev qt6-tools-dev
+apt install libkf6guiaddons-dev libqt6dbus6 libqt6network6 libqt6core6 libqt6widgets6 libqt6gui6 libqt6svg6 qt6-qpa-plugins
+```
+
+Fedora:
+
+```sh
+dnf install gcc-c++ cmake qt6-qtbase-devel qt6-qtsvg-devel qt6-qttools qt6-linguist qt6-qttools-devel kf6-kguiaddons-devel
+dnf install qt6-qtbase qt6-qtsvg kf6-kguiaddons
+```
+
+Arch Linux:
+
+```sh
+pacman -S cmake base-devel git qt6-base qt6-tools kguiaddons qt6-svg
+```
+
+Then configure and build:
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
+cmake --build build --parallel
+```
+
+The executable is produced under `build/src`. Packaging and installation can
+follow the normal upstream Flameshot instructions.
+
+## Features and default shortcuts
+
+| Feature | Shortcut | Behavior |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Cursor annotation | `U` | Inserts a deterministic vector mouse pointer that can be moved and resized. |
+| Smart Eraser | `E` | Samples the exact first-click source colour and paints with that immutable colour. |
+| Text Background | `Shift+T` | Editable text with font, foreground, background, opacity, padding, rounded corners, and automatic bounds. |
+| Visual Blur | `Shift+B` | Editable visual-only blur. This is not a secure redaction tool. |
+| Secure Pixelate | `Ctrl+Shift+P` | Irreversibly replaces the selected source region with a synthetic pixelated cover after confirmation. |
+| Secure Blur | `Ctrl+Shift+B` | Irreversibly replaces the selected source region with a synthetic soft cover after confirmation. |
+| Spotlight | `Shift+S` | Keeps an editable rectangular focus area bright while dimming the rest of the canvas. |
+| Freehand Selection | `L` | Draws a lasso crop. Pixels outside the path are transparent and output is forced to PNG. |
+
+Editable two-point annotations expose stable endpoint resize handles. A resize
+drag creates one undo step and preserves directional endpoints such as arrow
+start and end.
+
+Existing customized toolbars receive the new feature buttons once. A migration
+version is then recorded, so tools a user subsequently disables are not added
+back on later launches.
+
+## Screenshot-derived area selection
+
+With no active drawing tool and no committed selection, moving the pointer can
+show a dashed candidate rectangle inferred from visible screenshot borders.
+Click to accept a strong, unambiguous candidate. Drag more than the normal
+movement threshold to use the ordinary manual rectangle instead.
+
+Detection is deterministic and uses only pixels already returned by the
+screenshot provider. It does not enumerate compositor windows, query X11
+windows, or depend on an active-window API. Advanced confidence controls are
+available in the `[General]` section of `flameshot.ini`:
+
+```ini
+geometricAreaMinimumConfidence=42
+geometricAreaAmbiguityTolerance=6
+```
+
+Both values are percentages from 0 through 100.
+
+## Wayland and GNOME
+
+Wayland remains on Flameshot's existing XDG Desktop Portal screenshot path.
+Install and run the portal backend appropriate to the desktop; GNOME should use
+its GNOME portal backend. See the
+[XDG Screenshot portal interface](https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/doc-org.freedesktop.portal.Screenshot.html).
+
+The new geometric selector is intentionally screenshot-derived because GNOME
+Wayland does not provide ordinary clients with reliable foreign-window
+geometry. It therefore works with the portal-provided desktop image without a
+private GNOME Shell API or X11 compatibility dependency. Logical paths and
+rectangles are converted to physical half-open pixel bounds, including at
+fractional scale factors such as 125% and 150%.
+
+GNOME and COSMIC's delayed clipboard handoff remains in place. A transparent
+lasso advertises PNG data so the alpha mask survives the compositor clipboard
+transfer. JPEG is never selected for an output that actually contains
+transparent pixels.
+
+## X11
+
+Full desktop environments continue to use the XDG Screenshot portal by
+default, including on X11. The feature patch adds no XCB dependency and the
+geometric selector still analyzes only the returned screenshot. Existing
+minimal-window-manager users can keep using Flameshot's documented
+`useX11LegacyScreenshot=true` fallback; that option is ignored on Wayland.
+
+## Secure-redaction boundary
+
+Ordinary Pixelate and Visual Blur are presentation effects, not security
+features. Use only Secure Pixelate or Secure Blur when the covered pixels must
+not appear in exported image data.
+
+Confirming a secure redaction first flattens visible annotations, generates a
+source-independent synthetic cover, applies it to the full backing screenshot,
+and replaces the editor's original/current/magnifier caches. Annotation objects,
+undo history, redo history, and screenshot-derived selection snapshots are
+then rebuilt from the sanitized image. Output is blocked while a secure
+redaction is pending, cancelled, or cannot be applied.
+
+This is an application-level purge boundary. It does not claim physical RAM
+zeroization and cannot erase pixels already retained by the compositor,
+desktop portal, GPU, swap, clipboard manager, another process, or an earlier
+saved/exported image.
+
+The lasso stores geometry rather than a second pixel cache. Secure redaction is
+applied to the complete image before the final lasso crop and alpha mask.
+Outside pixels become fully transparent with zero hidden RGB; antialiased edge
+pixels can intentionally retain partially transparent source colour.
+
+## Scope decision
+
+OCR and QR decoding are not included. They would require extra dependencies or
+substantial platform integration and did not meet the patch's small,
+local-dependency goal.
+
+## Linux validation checklist
+
+This patch was source-reviewed and statically inspected only. At the request of
+the patch owner, it was not built or tested on the macOS development machine.
+Recipients should at minimum perform the following checks on Linux:
+
+1. Build both a normal Linux configuration and the distribution's intended
+   Wayland clipboard configuration.
+2. On GNOME Wayland, launch `flameshot gui` through the XDG Screenshot portal
+   and verify the overlay covers every monitor correctly.
+3. Repeat selection, copy, save, and open-app flows at 100%, 125%, and 150%
+   display scaling where available.
+4. Verify screenshot-derived candidates accept on click and fall back to a
+   manual rectangle on drag.
+5. Verify lasso copy/save preserves transparent pixels as PNG, including when
+   the global clipboard preference is JPEG.
+6. Begin a lasso and invoke copy/save before completing it; output must remain
+   blocked. Repeat with a too-small lasso, then cancel and draw a rectangle.
+7. Confirm each secure-redaction mode, then exercise copy, save, pin, upload,
+   raw-PNG, launcher, undo, and redo routes. No pre-confirmation source pixel
+   may reappear.
+8. Check multi-monitor negative origins and mixed/fractional scale factors.
+9. Repeat the core selection and export checks in an X11 session.
