@@ -135,14 +135,28 @@ Ordinary Pixelate and Visual Blur are presentation effects, not security
 features. Use only Secure Pixelate or Secure Blur when the covered pixels must
 not appear in exported image data.
 
-Confirming a secure redaction first flattens visible annotations, generates a
-source-independent synthetic cover, applies it to the full backing screenshot,
-and replaces the editor's original/current/magnifier caches. Annotation objects,
-undo history, redo history, and screenshot-derived selection snapshots are
-then rebuilt from the sanitized image. Output is blocked while a secure
-redaction is pending or cannot be applied. Cancelling the confirmation discards
-the unconfirmed descriptor and aborts that initiating action; a later export
-remains possible with the image unchanged.
+Confirming a secure redaction first installs a source-independent synthetic
+cover over the target on a clean backing image. Deep-copied annotations are
+then rendered only against that sanitized base; Smart Eraser's retained direct
+sample is replaced when its tracked source point falls inside the target. The
+secure cover is applied again last, and the result replaces the editor's
+original/current/magnifier caches.
+Annotation objects plus undo/redo history are cleared, while the
+screenshot-derived selection snapshot is rebuilt from the sanitized image.
+Output is blocked while a secure redaction is pending or cannot be applied.
+Cancelling the confirmation discards the unconfirmed descriptor and aborts
+that initiating action; a later export remains possible with the image
+unchanged.
+
+Visible annotations explicitly drawn outside the target remain part of the
+flattened image. The checkpoint prevents automatic blur, pixelate, invert, or
+Smart Eraser processing from directly sampling original target pixels into that
+exterior. This is a managed backing/effect-raster cache guarantee, not
+whole-application taint tracking. Existing Color Grab, manually chosen colours,
+and other explicit user-authored annotation content can reproduce information
+outside the target; their provenance is not tracked and that visible content
+is retained. This limitation also applies to transitive influence through
+annotations created before the confirmed checkpoint.
 
 This is an application-level purge boundary. It does not claim physical RAM
 zeroization and cannot erase pixels already retained by the compositor,
@@ -179,7 +193,9 @@ Recipients should at minimum perform the following checks on Linux:
